---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/intermediate/reproduction-in-humans">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Biology lesson. The foundational lessons that matter most here are
    <strong>DNA and heredity</strong> (chromosomes, and why gametes must be haploid) and
    <strong>human body systems</strong> (blood, hormones and glands). The intermediate lesson on
    inheritance patterns deepens the meiosis story but is not required first.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>compare sexual and asexual reproduction, and say what each costs and buys;</li>
    <li>match the structures of the human reproductive systems to their jobs;</li>
    <li>follow fertilisation from sperm meeting egg to implantation;</li>
    <li>explain the placenta as an exchange surface, and what crosses it;</li>
    <li>describe the menstrual cycle's hormones and how contraception and IVF press the same switches.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>The idea: two strategies, one goal</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Asexual reproduction</strong> — one parent, genetically identical offspring (clones)
    — is fast, cheap and safe when the environment suits you: bacteria divide every 20 minutes,
    strawberry plants send out runners, fungi pour out spores. <strong>Sexual reproduction</strong>
    — two parents, gametes, genetic recombination — is slower and costlier, and it dominates the
    complex, changing world because the variation it generates is insurance against parasites,
    predators and shifting climates (the genetics lesson traced the mechanism through meiosis).
    Humans, like nearly all animals, are committed to the sexual route — and so we carry
    specialised anatomy, a hormone-driven monthly programme, and a nine-month investment in each
    offspring. That programme is what this lesson follows, from gamete to implantation to
    newborn. Note the trade in its purest form: an asexual lineage doubles its numbers every
    generation; a sexual lineage spends half its reproduction making offspring that carry only
    half of each parent's genes. The payoff — recombination — only shows its worth across
    generations under attack from rapidly evolving parasites and diseases, which is exactly the
    world complex organisms inhabit.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "asexual reproduction is a primitive mistake that evolution
  abandoned." It is anything but: an asexual lineage doubles its numbers every generation and
  never needs a partner, which is why bacteria and many plants still run it. Sex persists
  despite its cost because variation is insurance in a world of fast-evolving parasites — a
  trade, not a tournament with a single winner.</p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="true or false — two strategies, one goal" widget="judge" config={rpStrategyJudge} />

  <h2>Two gametes, built differently</h2>
  <p>
    Sexual reproduction fuses two specialised cells, and each is engineered for its job. The
    <strong>sperm</strong> is a delivery vehicle: a <strong>flagellum</strong> for swimming, a
    midpiece packed with <strong>mitochondria</strong> to power it, and <strong>acrosome
    enzymes</strong> in the head to cut through the egg's defences — tiny (about 0.05 mm long),
    motile, and released in their millions (a typical ejaculate contains 40–300 million; only a
    few hundred survive the journey far enough to reach the egg). The <strong>egg cell</strong>
    is the opposite bet: about <strong>0.1–0.2 mm</strong> across (just visible to the naked
    eye), immobile, <strong>stocked with energy stores</strong> and wrapped in a
    <strong>jelly coat</strong> that changes at fertilisation to lock out competitors. One egg
    is released per cycle; the sperm deliver a fleet. Compare them by size, structure, motility
    and numbers and you have a favourite exam question — but the deeper point is economics.
    Sperm are cheap to make and expendable by design; eggs are precious and defended. That
    asymmetry, repeated across nearly all animal species, shapes mating behaviour everywhere.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="pair each structure with its engineering job" widget="match" config={rpGameteMatch} />
  <p>
    Both gametes are <strong>haploid</strong> — 23 chromosomes — produced by meiosis, and their
    fusion restores the diploid 46. Sperm are made continuously in the <strong>testes</strong>
    from puberty onward (about 1,500 per second in a healthy male); eggs are different: all the
    egg precursor cells a female will ever have exist before birth, and after puberty one
    <strong>follicle</strong> — an egg plus its nursing cells — matures each month in the
    <strong>ovary</strong>. In both sexes the gametes' DNA contribution is only half the story:
    sperm carry almost no cytoplasm, so nearly all the mitochondria and nutrients of the first
    cells of a new life come from the egg — which is why mitochondrial DNA is always inherited
    from the mother. The anatomy is arranged for these two gamete streams to meet: testes held
    in the scrotum a degree or two below core temperature (sperm production fails at body
    temperature — a design constraint visible every morning), vas deferens carrying sperm
    through the prostate, which adds fluid; ovaries releasing eggs into the open funnel of the
    oviduct, which sweeps them toward the uterus by cilia and muscular peristalsis, the same
    tube in which fertilisation will occur.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="call the magnitude — the sperm production line" widget="estimate" config={rpSpermEstimate} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> two differently engineered gametes are made — millions of cheap,
    motile sperm, one stocked egg — and the anatomy delivers them to a single meeting place.
    What happens when they meet is a six-step relay.
  </p>

  <h2>Fertilisation → implantation</h2>
  <p>
    Fertilisation is the <strong>fusion of the nuclei</strong> of sperm and egg, and it happens
    in the <strong>oviduct</strong>. What follows is a six-step relay from encounter to a
    settled pregnancy:
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from a million candidates to one implanted embryo" widget="equationStep" config={fertCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the six stages and watch two themes. First,
    filtering: millions of candidates are narrowed until a single sperm contributes its
    nucleus. Second, blocking: the moment one sperm enters, the egg hardens against all
    others, because a polyspermic zygote cannot develop. Fertilisation is a gate, not a
    finish line.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="find the faulty line — the fertilisation relay" widget="spot-error" config={rpPolySpot} />

  <p>
    Once implanted, the embryo's <strong>placenta</strong> and <strong>umbilical cord</strong>
    take over exchange: dissolved nutrients and oxygen pass from mother's blood to fetus's,
    and urea and carbon dioxide pass back — the two bloods stay separate, but substances
    diffuse across. The placenta is an exchange surface built to the same four-point spec as
    the lung: huge area (villi tree into a blood-filled space), thin barrier, both sides
    well-perfused, flow continuously renewed. The <strong>amniotic sac</strong> and fluid
    cushion the fetus against knocks and keep its temperature even. One warning deserves its
    own sentence: <strong>some pathogens and toxins can cross the placenta</strong> — alcohol,
    nicotine, and viruses such as rubella and HIV among them. The fetus shares the mother's
    blood chemistry, which is why "eating for two" is really "filtering for two": whatever the
    mother takes in, the placenta offers to the fetus. The exchange is efficient enough that
    by full term the placenta delivers the fetus about 25 mL of oxygen per minute and clears
    the corresponding CO₂ — a tiny lung, working continuously, discarded at birth.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="file the claims — crosses the placenta or not" widget="sort-buckets" config={rpPlacentaBuckets} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the baby breathes and eats through the umbilical cord, and
    the mother's blood mixes with the fetus's." The cord is plumbing between fetus and
    placenta, and the placenta is the only exchange site — no direct mixing, no breathing, no
    swallowing into a stomach that matters yet. The fetal lungs are collapsed and bypassed by a
    shunt; the placenta does their job, exchanging gases exactly as the lung will after birth.
    At birth the shunts close and the lungs take over within the first breaths — one of the
    most abrupt plumbing changes in biology.</p>

  <h2>Hormones and the ~28-day cycle</h2>
  <p>
    At puberty, <strong>testosterone</strong> in males and <strong>oestrogen</strong> in
    females drive the development of secondary sexual characteristics — deepening voice, body
    hair, growth spurt, breast development. In females, the same hormones run the
    <strong>menstrual cycle</strong>: about every 28 days the uterus lining is shed (days 1–5),
    then rebuilds, thickens with blood vessels, and — if no embryo implants — is shed again.
    The hormone cast: <strong>FSH</strong> from the pituitary matures a follicle; the
    follicle's <strong>oestrogen</strong> rebuilds the uterine lining and, once high enough,
    triggers the <strong>LH</strong> surge that causes <strong>ovulation</strong> (about day 14
    in a 28-day cycle); the emptied follicle becomes the <strong>corpus luteum</strong>, whose
    <strong>progesterone</strong> keeps the lining maintained in case of pregnancy. If no
    embryo signals, the corpus luteum dies about two weeks on, progesterone collapses, and the
    lining is shed — the cycle restarts. Note the feedback shape: oestrogen rising triggers
    LH (positive feedback, the one exception in endocrinology), while progesterone suppresses
    further FSH (negative feedback) — the two feedback types working in the same cycle.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="rebuild the 28-day cycle's hormone relay" widget="order" config={rpCycleOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "ovulation always lands on day 14 of every cycle." Day 14
  belongs to the textbook 28-day cycle; the luteal phase stays near 14 days while the phase
  before ovulation absorbs almost all the variation, so a 26-day cycle ovulates near day 12
  and a 32-day cycle near day 18. Counting forward from a period is the unreliable part.</p>
  <p>
    Progesterone leaves a trace you can measure at home: it raises basal body temperature by
    about <strong>0.3–0.5 °C</strong> after ovulation, and the fall just before menstruation
    is what lets cycle-tracking apps guess your ovulation day:
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="pin the post-ovulatory plateau on the thermometer" widget="place" config={rpBBTPlace} />

  <Widget no="2" caption="basal body temperature across a cycle — the step up is progesterone's signature after ovulation (~day 14)" widget="plotter" config={bbtCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the curve stays nearly flat until about day 14, then steps
    up a fraction of a degree and holds. That step is progesterone's signature — the corpus
    luteum warming the body after ovulation. Slide the post-ovulatory rise to see how small a
    signal you are hunting for, which is why cycle tracking demands a good thermometer and
    daily patience.
  </p>

  <p>
    Timing is everything in reproduction, because the gametes' clocks do not match — sperm
    survive up to about <strong>5 days</strong> in the female tract, the egg only about
    <strong>12–24 hours</strong>. Move the cycle length and watch the fertile window shift
    (the luteal phase stays close to 14 days even when cycles vary):
  </p>

  <Widget no="3" caption="the fertile window of any cycle length — it opens days before ovulation, not on it" widget="sliderLab" config={labCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> move the cycle length between 26 and 32 days and watch all
    three readouts shift together. The fertile window opens several days <em>before</em>
    ovulation, not on it, because sperm can wait up to five days while the egg lasts only
    12–24 hours. The luteal phase — ovulation to period — barely moves; the earlier phases take
    all the strain.
  </p>

  <p>
    The same hormone logic powers both <strong>contraception</strong> and
    <strong>fertility treatment</strong>. The combined pill supplies oestrogen and progesterone
    at steady levels, which — through negative feedback — stops FSH from rising and thus
    prevents any follicle maturing: no egg, nothing to fertilise. The morning-after pill loads
    progesterone high enough to delay or prevent ovulation. IVF runs the loop in reverse:
    fertility drugs raise FSH to mature several follicles at once, eggs are collected,
    fertilised in a dish, and one or two embryos are placed back into a progesterone-primed
    uterus. Both technologies are just the menstrual cycle's own feedback switches, pressed
    deliberately. Condoms, by contrast, work mechanically — and, uniquely among contraceptives,
    also block STI transmission.
  </p>
  <Widget no="11" caption="press the switches — contraception or IVF" widget="branch" config={rpTechBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the same feedback loop runs the cycle and the technologies — the
    pill suppresses FSH, IVF amplifies it, and tracking reads progesterone's temperature trace.
    Sexually transmitted infections share a route, not a species; the next section follows
    that route and how to cut it.
  </p>

  <h2>STIs and HIV</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>sexually transmitted infection</strong> is one passed by sexual contact — the
    pathogen's route, not its species, defines it. <strong>HIV</strong> is a virus that causes
    such an infection and <strong>may lead to AIDS</strong>, in which the immune system is too
    damaged to fight infections it would once have shrugged off (the immune system lesson
    dissects the mechanism: HIV destroys the helper T cells that coordinate everything).
    Transmission is by body fluids: unprotected sex, shared needles, unscreened blood
    products, and from mother to child across the placenta or in breast milk — <em>not</em>
    by everyday contact, sharing food, or insect bites. Controlling the spread needs no new
    biology, only barriers and behaviour: condoms (a physical barrier to the pathogen),
    screening donated blood, sterile needles, treating infected mothers so the
    virus never reaches the child, and education. Antiretroviral drugs do not cure HIV but
    hold it in check, which is why AIDS, once a death sentence, is now a manageable condition
    in treated populations. The same logic — stop the vector, break the chain — controls all
    STI spread, from chlamydia (treatable, often symptomless, and a leading cause of
    infertility when missed) to syphilis and gonorrhoea. The public-health pattern is worth
    stating explicitly: STIs spread along networks, not populations at random, so the
    effective interventions are the ones that cut transmission probability per contact
    (condoms, treatment) and the duration of infectiousness (testing and early treatment) —
    both are arithmetic on the reproduction number you met in the immune system lesson's herd
    immunity discussion.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why is the oviduct the only place fertilisation can happen, and where does implantation occur?</li>
    <li>A woman's cycle is 32 days. When did she probably ovulate, and why not on day 14?</li>
    <li>Why does the combined pill prevent any follicle from maturing?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The oviduct is where the swept-up egg waits and where sperm
    meet it; the six-step relay ends with the embryo implanting in the uterus lining about a
    week later. (2) Near day 18 — the luteal phase is fixed at about 14 days before the next
    period, so ovulation is found by counting backwards. (3) Steady oestrogen and progesterone
    from the pill hold FSH down through negative feedback, so no follicle matures and no egg
    is released.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sexual reproduction buys genetic variation at half the reproductive throughput; asexual reproduction is fast but uniform.</li>
    <li>Sperm are cheap and motile; eggs are few, stocked and defended — an asymmetry repeated across the animal kingdom.</li>
    <li>Fertilisation happens in the oviduct and ends with implantation in the uterus lining.</li>
    <li>The placenta is a lung-like exchange surface; the two bloods stay separate while substances diffuse across.</li>
    <li>FSH, oestrogen, LH and progesterone run the cycle through one positive and one negative feedback loop.</li>
    <li>Contraception and IVF press the cycle's own feedback switches in opposite directions.</li>
    <li>STIs are defined by transmission route; barriers and early treatment cut the chain.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling intermediate lesson <strong>the immune system</strong> takes over exactly where
    the STI section stops — how HIV dismantles the helper T cells that coordinate everything —
    and advanced developmental biology follows the zygote from here onward.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The cycle's precision is measurable. Ovulation day is best predicted not by counting from
    the start but by counting backwards — the luteal phase (ovulation to menstruation) is
    nearly fixed at 14 days, while the follicular phase (period to ovulation) absorbs almost
    all the variation, which is why a 26-day cycle ovulates near day 12 and a 32-day cycle near
    day 18. The LH surge itself is a sharp peak lasting about 24–36 hours, and modern ovulation
    test strips simply detect it in urine — a home assay for a pituitary hormone. Pregnancy
    tests run the same trick on hCG, the hormone the implanting embryo starts secreting within
    days of implantation; it is structurally similar enough to LH that it keeps the corpus
    luteum alive, which is exactly its purpose: stop the progesterone collapse, keep the
    lining, buy the placenta time to form.
  </p>
  <p>
    The numbers of early pregnancy are stark. Of the eggs fertilised in any given cycle — and
    fertilisation itself is estimated to succeed for perhaps only a third of ovulations even in
    healthy couples — a large fraction fail to implant or are lost within the first weeks,
    often before the pregnancy is detectable. Of the sperm that start the journey, roughly one
    in a million reaches the egg; the rest are lost to acidity, mucus selection, wrong turns
    and time. This culling is not pathology but the system working: gamete quality control at
    the level of populations rather than checkpoints. The remaining open questions are active
    research: how sperm navigate the final stretch (chemotaxis to egg-secreted signals is
    demonstrated in mammals but not fully mapped), how the maternal immune system tolerates a
    fetus that is half foreign tissue (a partial, regulated immunosuppression unique to the
    placenta), and why human births are so difficult — the compromise between a narrow pelvis
    shaped for walking and a fetal skull shaped for brains, a tension that ensures no human
    birth is ever quite routine.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Biology 0610 syllabus (2026–2028), unit 16; OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology, ch. 27–28 (Reproductive System; Development); WHO laboratory manual for the examination of human semen, 6th ed. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
